Tim Ramsey <tar@matt.ksu.ksu.edu> wrote:
>
>On another note, how about expiring articles earlier based on what groups
>they were posted to?  What if a state university expires soc.* in two days
>but expires comp.* in 10?  Or alt.sex* in 1?  Is that a form of
>censorship?
>

Well, this can be done, but not without some unhappy folks.

If you're going to take a feed of a certain set of newsgroups, there's
only one real way to respect users' wishes with expiration times -- base
them on readership.  Collect up all the .newsrc files and simply count
the readers of each group.  The groups with fewer readers get shorter
expiration times; the greater the number of readers, the longer the
articles should remain online.

Of course, there will be those who argue that the admin has no business
whatsoever examining users' .newsrc files...although the arbitron pro-
gram will do so without revealing individual readers' preferences.

Of course, you could base expiration on traffic; the group that turns
in XXX Mb/day will expire sooner than the one that turns in YY Mb/day.
This is somewhat unwieldly, since a good discussion can cause a drama-
tic surge in volume.  It also tends to ignore users' preferences.

Just out of curiosity, how many readers here would agree to a plan
that based expiration times on number of readers?

--Wes

