NEWS RELEASE
Attorney-General The Hon. Michael Lavarch MP
26/93
24 June 1993

GOVERNMENTS ACT ON VIDEO GAME CONCERNS

The Federal, State and Territory governments agreed today to bring video
and computer games under the control of the national censorship system.

The Standing Committee of Censorship Misters, meeting in Darwin, expressed
concern at the prospect that violent and sexually explicit material in
video games available overseas could come onto the Australian market
unless there was a system of regulation.

The Ministers asked the Chief Censor and State and Territory officials to
begin discussions immediately with industry and community groups to
formulate a scheme for regulation to be presented at the next meeting in
October.

The meeting viewed a presentation of computer games demonstrating the
variety of video games on the market at the moment and heard a report
outlining the technological developments likely with these games over the
next five years.

Ministers were told that this industry had grown in the seven years since
its inception to a turnover of $7 billion worldwide. The major
distributors of video games in Australia had already indicated that they
were prepared to cooperate with Government in a regulatory scheme to
differentiate between games for children and those considered more
appropriate for mature players. Unsuitable material would not be permitted
to be distributed in Australia.

In deciding to apply a system of regulation the Ministers considered the
availability of games through retail outlets and in arcades and their
availability from outlets around the country. They also considered the
problems with regulation of video games and other material by way of
bulletin boards both local and international.

The Ministers requested the Chief Censor to begin urgent discussions with
the industry to ensure that their interim self-regulatory code meets
current community standards. In addition, because of the nature of the
computer games, the Ministers also asked for input into the proposals from
behavioural and medical experts on the effect such material may have on
children and adolescents.

The Ministers agreed that proposals by the Law Reform Commission for a
single Censorship Code should he presented to them in the form of drafting
instructions for legislation. They would consider these drafting
instructions at their next meeting.

The meeting agreed that the drafting instructions should include a
partially compulsory scheme for the classification of literature in the
`adult' category. Material in this category would include publications
which had a preoccupation with sex, nudity, violence and drugs and which
instructed in matters of crime or violence. 

The new scheme would involve all publications which came within this
category being submitted to the Office of Film and Literature
Classification (OFLC) for classification. These publications would then be
given an official classification label by the OTIC for `Unrestricted',
`Category 1' or `Category 2', All publications which are required to be
submitted for classification would have to display those classification
categories or risk penalty.

The meeting also asked the OFLC to investigate the possibility of a
uniform scheme for the display of `Category 1' material in retail outlets
throughout Australia.

