Let me try to make sure that my argument is clear:

   As a matter of custom and practice, Netnews allows many kinds of
   copying. Reposting is very similar to many of these and no
   principled distinction can be made between reposting and other
   types of generally accepted copying.

   Some of these kinds of copying are:

    * Aliasing one newsgroup name to another, which like reposting has
            the effect of having an article appear under a newsgroup
            name that the article's author didn't specify.
    * Saving netnews articles to an FTP archive, which like reposting
            has the effects of changing the specific forum in which
            the article appears and of making it hard for the original
            author to "cancel" the article. Also, an archivist may
            only keep some articles (say the ones he or she thinks are
            legal or interesting), which, like reposting, has the
            effect of highlighting (without deep comment) some
            articles with respect to other articles.
    * On one's own newserver, not retrieving articles from a site that
            you don't like, which like reposting has the effect of
            highlighting (without deep comment) some articles with
            respect to other articles.
    * Creating a feed for high schools for rec.humor.clean that is
            just rec.humor less the articles crossposted to alt.sex*
            which like reposting has the effect of highlighting
            (without deep comment) some articles with respect to other
            articles and which like reposting has the effect of having
            an article appear under a newsgroup that the article's
            author didn't specify.
    * Including much of a post in a followup perhaps to another
            newsgroup which like reposting has the effect of
            highlighting some articles with respect to other articles
            and which like reposting has the effect of having the text
            of an article appear under a newsgroup that the article's
            author didn't specify. Also, like reposting, it has the
            effects of making it hard for the original author to
            "cancel" the article. Although the follow up often
            includes additional comment, the length and depth of this
            comment has always been at the discretion of the 2nd
            poster.

