In article <CE31Cs.3p8@ms.uky.edu>
	morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) says:
>If you're going to take a feed of a certain set of newsgroups, there's
>only one real way to respect users' wishes with expiration times -- base
>them on readership.  Collect up all the .newsrc files and simply count
>the readers of each group.  The groups with fewer readers get shorter
>expiration times; the greater the number of readers, the longer the
>articles should remain online.

This doesn't take in to account group volume.  Your scheme would have
low volume, low readership groups expire very quickly to leave room for
lots of high volume, high readership groups.  If I read a lot of low
volume groups I don't get my fair share of the spool.

The problem is using time as a measure to control a space limited
system.  So let me propose a slightly more complicated system that
works toward the same goal by making sure that each user gets a fair
ammount of disk space allocated to his or her groups.

Consider a site that has 1GB of available news spool and 500 readers.
Assign each user a "quota" of 1/500th of the total spool space...so
each user gets 2MB of quota.

Now suppose that I subscribe to 100 groups (what the hell, I've got
nothing better to do).  That means I have 20KB of my quota to give to
each group.

Since you subscribe to some of the SF groups you only have time to read
10 groups.  That means you have 200KB of quota to give to each group.

Add these quotas up and expire each group until it fits in the space
allocated to it.   High volume, high readership groups get a lot of
space while low volume low readership groups get a less space but
(because they are low volume) may be able to store a long period in
that space.   And each user can "claim" a fair share of the spool.
It seems to do a nice job of considering both readership and group
volume.

This system sounds complicated, but the calculations really wouldn't
require much work beyond what Wes' system requires.  The only
complication is in the expire step since expire uses a time base sheme
rather than a space base scheme.
