[ WARNING -- *extremely* off-the-cuff ideas follow.  Feel free to ]
[            munch upon them; I'm not expert with the news soft-  ]
[            ware, so I don't know how difficult this implementa- ]
[            tion would be.                                       ]

Of course, we'd have to set an upper limit of some sort.  We could also 
make an adjustment for volume.

What if we set up something like:

	- The maximum expiration time (MaxExp) is X days.
	- An arbitron program can determine the percentage of
	  readers (ReaderPercent) who read newsgroup a.b.c.
	- The base expiration time (BaseExp) given to newsgroup a.b.c is:
		BaseExp = (MaxExp) * (ReaderPercent)	
	- This base expiration is adjusted against the percentage of spool 
	  space occupied by the newsgroup in question (SpoolPercent), to 
	  yield the adjusted expiration time (AdjExp).  Since we want to
	  compensate for high volume, let's multiply like so:
		AdjExp = (BaseExp) * (1-SpoolPercent)

So, let's say that our absolute maximum expiration is 10 days.  Let's say
that newsgroup soc.whine.loud is read by 50 percent of our readers, and
that it occupies 25% of our spool space.  We wind up with:

	AdjExp = 10 * .5 * .75 = 5 * .75 = 4 days (rounded up from 3.75)

If newsgroup talk.argue.polite is read by 80 percent of the users and takes
up only 2% of spool space, we get:

	AdjExp = 10 * .8 * .98 = 8 * .98 = 8 days (rounded up from 7.84)

Of course, I'd guess that SpoolPercent would have to be calculated on
a daily basis; ReaderPercent could probably be done weekly.

If any single newsgroup occupies more than, say, 8-10% of spool space,
I think we would be justified in removing it from this scheme and
assigning a fixed expiration time.

Comments?  Feel free to shred this, folks; it's just off the top of my
head...

